The restrict qualifier of the compiler in use, or nothing.
It goes where \f[CR]restrict\f[R] goes, after the \f[CR]*\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
void    COPY(BYTE *RESTRICT DESTINATION, const BYTE *RESTRICT SOURCE, LET SIZE);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS restrict
.IP
.EX
#define restrict
.EE
.PP
Defined as \f[CR]RESTRICT\f[R] only where \f[CR]restrict\f[R] is not a
keyword (C89 and C++), so code written for C99 also compiles there.
.PP
Never defined on MSVC: its own headers say
\f[CR]__declspec(restrict)\f[R], which a \f[CR]restrict\f[R] macro would
break.
Use \f[CR]RESTRICT\f[R] there.
.PP
C99 and later are never touched.
.PP
